<ResourceDictionary xmlns:x="http://adamantium/ui/xaml/extensions"
        xmlns="http://adamantium/ui">

    <!-- The theme's MATERIALS - surfaces that are made of what is behind them rather than of a colour.

         THE KEYS NAME A ROLE, NOT A MATERIAL: FlyoutSurfaceFill is "whatever a menu, a drop-down, a ribbon flyout or a
         slide panel is made of in this theme", and every template asks for it by that name. They used to be called
         AcrylicFlyoutFill and AcrylicTooltipFill, which made the material part of the contract - so the macOS theme,
         which answers these with liquid glass, either had to lie in the name or re-template four controls it borrows
         wholesale to change one surface. A theme answering a role with a different material is the entire point of the
         keys being shared.

         WHY ONLY THE TRANSIENT SURFACES USE THIS. Acrylic takes a copy of the frame beneath it EVERY FRAME, which is
         affordable while a menu is open and is not affordable for a window's whole chrome. That is also why real
         Fluent draws the line where it does: acrylic for what appears and light-dismisses - menus, drop-downs,
         flyouts, tooltips - and mica, which reads the wallpaper once and follows neither scroll nor content, for the
         long-lived background. The two are not interchangeable and picking by looks rather than by lifetime is how a
         theme ends up costing a capture per control.

         The solid keys these replace (CardBackgroundFillColorDefault and friends) stay in the palette: they are the
         fallback for a platform with no capture, and going back is one attribute.

         The TINT comes from the palette so the variants keep their contract - light and dark supply a colour, the
         brush itself belongs to the theme. -->

    <!-- The flyout surface: menus, submenus, drop-down lists, the ribbon's flyout, a colour picker's popup.
         Tint at 0.78 rather than the sandbox's 0.55, and that is a legibility floor, not a taste: a menu can open over
         anything at all, including a white document, and its text has to stay readable over the worst of them.
         The blur is what makes it a SURFACE and not a stain: too little and the content beneath stays readable through
         the panel, which looks like a bug rather than like a material. The number is the reach of the Gaussian's
         outermost tap in frame pixels, over a copy already shrunk fourfold - so it is a good deal stronger than the
         same number meant under the old point-sampled kernels, and it does not need to be large. -->
    <MaterialBrush x:Key="FlyoutSurfaceFill"
                   Material="Acrylic"
                   TintColor="{ResourceReference AcrylicFillColorDefault}"
                   TintOpacity="0.78"
                   BlurAmount="4"
                   NoiseAmount="0.03"/>

    <!-- The same material, thinner, for surfaces that sit CLOSE to what they cover and want to be felt through:
         a tooltip, which is small enough that a heavy tint reads as a black box. -->
    <MaterialBrush x:Key="TooltipSurfaceFill"
                   Material="Acrylic"
                   TintColor="{ResourceReference AcrylicFillColorDefault}"
                   TintOpacity="0.70"
                   BlurAmount="3"
                   NoiseAmount="0.03"/>

</ResourceDictionary>
